<h2>What the section says, and what it assumes</h2>
<p>Section 21 opens with a non-obstante clause — &ldquo;notwithstanding anything contained in any other law for the time being in force&rdquo; — and then confers the power: at any stage of the hearing of an application for a protection order or any other relief under the Act, the Magistrate may grant temporary custody of any child or children to the aggrieved person, and may, if necessary, make arrangements for visit of such child or children by the respondent. The proviso permits outright refusal of visits where they may harm the child&rsquo;s interests. Three design features follow. The remedy is interlocutory, available &ldquo;at any stage&rdquo; and without a separate suit. It is asymmetric: custody can be granted to the aggrieved person, with the respondent&rsquo;s position addressed through visitation. And it is welfare-driven: the proviso&rsquo;s test is the child&rsquo;s interest, not the parents&rsquo; equities.</p>

<h2>How a Section 21 application is actually fought</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The foundation is the DV application itself.</strong> Section 21 is not freestanding; it operates within proceedings under Section 12. The domestic incident report, the specific acts of violence pleaded, and the children&rsquo;s exposure to them form the evidentiary backdrop.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Welfare material decides it.</strong> School records showing which parent manages education, medical records, the child&rsquo;s age and attachment, and any history of violence witnessed by the child matter more than the parties&rsquo; mutual accusations.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Visitation is the usual middle ground.</strong> Magistrates commonly grant custody to the mother with structured visitation — supervised or in a public setting where apprehensions exist, graduating with time. An order refusing all contact requires a distinct, child-focused justification under the proviso.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Modification remains available.</strong> Under Section 25, orders under the Act can be altered or revoked on a change of circumstances, so a Section 21 arrangement is never frozen.</div>
</div>

<h2>Section 21 beside the other custody forums</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Forum</th><th>Nature</th><th>Time horizon</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 21, DV Act (Magistrate)</td><td>Temporary custody and visitation during DV proceedings</td><td>Immediate; interim by design</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 26, HMA (Family Court)</td><td>Interim and final orders about children in matrimonial proceedings</td><td>Tied to the matrimonial case</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Guardians &amp; Wards Act, 1890 (Family Court)</td><td>Full guardianship and custody adjudication</td><td>The long-term determination</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The practical significance of Section 21 is speed and venue. A woman who has left the matrimonial home with the children and filed a DV application does not need to open a second front immediately; the Magistrate already seized of her case can stabilise the children&rsquo;s position. Conversely, a respondent facing a one-sided interim arrangement should engage with the DV proceedings on the welfare question — and, where the long-term stakes justify it, institute guardianship proceedings where both parents stand on an equal statutory footing.</p>

<h2>Common errors on both sides</h2>
<p>Aggrieved persons sometimes treat Section 21 orders as permanent victories and resist all visitation reflexively; courts respond poorly to the weaponising of children, and an unjustified refusal of contact can colour the eventual guardianship inquiry. Respondents, for their part, sometimes bypass the order — collecting children from school, extending visits unilaterally. That conduct invites modification of the arrangement against them and, where a protection order also exists, can amount to its breach, which Section 31 makes a criminal offence. The safe course on both sides is the same: comply, document, and seek modification through the court.</p>
<div class="note">This article is general information on the statutory scheme, not advice in any matter. Custody outcomes depend entirely on the welfare assessment on the specific record, and no result can be assumed from the framework described here.</div>
